## Pattern examples

Use these examples to calibrate your scoring. Each key matches a criterion from the scoring table.

---

### THIN_ACCOUNT_0 — author has 0–1 total reviews

> "Very satisfied, would recommend." — 0 reviews total
> "Everything was exactly as expected, will return." — 1 review

---

### THIN_ACCOUNT_1 — author has 2–3 total reviews

> "Excellent quality, will return." — 2 reviews total, both 5★
> "Everything was exactly as expected, will return." — 3 review total, nothing else on the profile — 1-2 reviews total all 5★

---

### SHORT_TEXT_EXTREME — text under 20 characters

> "Excellent!"
> "Top service."

---

### SHORT_TEXT — text under 60 characters

> "Fast and quality work, thank you."
> "Brought my car for an oil change, quick and easy." — 3 reviews, all in the same month
> "Great guys, fair price, no issues."

---

### BURST_AFTER_NEGATIVE — 5★ review immediately follows a 1–2★ review

> "Completely ruined my car. Avoid at all costs." (1★, position 7)
> "Best workshop I've ever visited! Exceptional service!!" (5★, position 8) ← burst
> "Amazing service, top quality work!" (5★, position 9) ← burst

---

### FIVE_STAR_EMPTY — 5★ with text under 50 characters

> "Great service, highly recommend!" (5★)
> "Five stars, no question at all." (5★)

---

### SURNAME_REPEAT — multiple reviewers share the same last name

> Jan Kowalski: "Great service!" (5★)
> Piotr Kowalski: "Very professional." (5★)
> Anna Kowalski: "Recommended!" (5★)
> Анна Ковальска: Супер!
> Иван Ковальски: Еще лучше

---

### GENERIC_TEXT — absence of specifics

**Signal: the review contains zero verifiable facts.**
Ask: what was repaired? what was bought? who helped? how much? how long?
If none of these can be answered from the text → `GENERIC_TEXT`.

Note: `GENERIC_TEXT` and `MARKETING_TONE` are **separate signals** and can both apply to the same review.
- `GENERIC_TEXT` = no facts
- `MARKETING_TONE` = exaggerated emotional tone

**A real review contains at least one of:**
- what was repaired / what product was bought / what service was performed
- a specific price or price range ("charged €120", "paid 800 PLN")
- a waiting time ("waited 40 minutes", "ready same day")
- a staff name or physical detail ("thanks to Marek", "the waiting room has free coffee")
- a concrete problem described ("the brakes were grinding", "engine warning light was on")

**Short obvious cases:**
> "The service was great and I am very happy with the result."
> "Everything was great, thank you for the work."

**Longer tricky cases — natural language, decent length, but still zero real detail:**
> "Great auto service. The guys know their job — everything was done quickly, professionally and without any fuss. Pleasant attitude towards customers and fair prices. From now on our whole family will service our cars only here. Highly recommend!"
> ← sounds authentic, but: no repair type, no price, no staff name, no waiting time.

> "Excellent workshop with a great atmosphere. Came in with a problem, left with everything sorted. The staff were polite and efficient. Strongly recommend this place to anyone looking for quality car service in the area."
> ← "came in with a problem" — what problem? No answer.

---

### SUSPICIOUS_NEGATIVE — 1–2★ with thin account, short text, or generic content

> "Terrible, never again." (author: 1 review, 22 chars)
> "Awful service, waste of money!" (author: 0 reviews, no details about what happened)
> "Would not recommend to anyone." (no specific incident, no description)

---

### MARKETING_TONE — promotional or exaggerated language

**Signal: the review reads like an advertisement, not a personal experience.**
Key markers: superlatives without substance, emotional filler, generic recommendations.

> "This is truly the best auto repair shop in the entire region, unmatched quality!"
> "Incredible professionals offering a unique and unbeatable service experience."
> "World-class results at an amazing price — there is simply no better option."
> "Absolutely outstanding — I have never experienced anything like this before!"
> "A hidden gem! The most talented team I have ever encountered, period."

---

### ALL_POSITIVE — only praise, zero negatives or caveats

**Signal: not a single critical or neutral note in the entire review.**
Real customers almost always mention at least something: a wait, a minor inconvenience, a caveat.

> "Everything was perfect — fast, professional, friendly, great price. Couldn't ask for more!"
> "The team is amazing, the work is flawless, the price is unbeatable, the service is top notch."
> "Fantastic in every way. The best experience I have ever had at any workshop."

---

### NO_FIRST_PERSON — no "I / me / my / we / я / мы"

> "Good service. Fast. Quality. The mechanics know their craft."
> "The team is experienced and the results speak for themselves. No complaints whatsoever. Definitely worth visiting if you need reliable car service in this area."

---

### FUTURE_PROMISE — vague future commitment without describing what happened

**Signal: promises to return or recommend, but never says what was done, fixed, or experienced.**
Research shows fake reviews disproportionately use future tense as a substitute for real experience.

> "Will definitely be back! Highly recommend to everyone."
> "This will be my go-to place from now on. Can't recommend enough."
> "This is now my go-to place. Recommend to everyone!"

---

### BUSINESS_NAME_DROP — explicitly names the business in the review text

> "Thank you Old School Custom Classic Garage for the amazing service!"
> "I recommend the place to absolutely everyone!"
> "If you're looking for the best service, The place is the only place you need. The team there is unbeatable and I won't go anywhere else."

---

### EMOJI_SPAM — excessive punctuation or emoji with no substance

> "Amazing!!! 🔥🔥🔥 Loved it so much!!!! 💯💯"
> "WOW!!!! Best ever!!!! ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐🙌🙌🙌"

---

### REPEATABLE_PLUSES_2 — exactly 2 distinct praise phrases

> "Amazing food, wonderful atmosphere." (2 praises: amazing food / wonderful atmosphere)
> "The staff was so kind and the room was spotless." (2 praises: kind staff / spotless room)

---

### REPEATABLE_PLUSES_3 — 3 or more distinct praise phrases

> "Incredible food, amazing service, and a beautiful interior. Highly recommended!" (4 praises)
> "The staff were fantastic, the prices were great, and the experience was outstanding." (3 praises)

---

### TEMPLATE_CLONE — phrasing nearly identical to another review

> "Fast service, professional staff, highly recommend to everyone."
> "Fast service, professional team, highly recommend to all."
> "Fast diagnosis, professional approach, recommend without hesitation."

---

### LONG_TEXT — review over 400 characters (real signal)

> "I brought my car in for brake work on a Tuesday morning. The mechanic, Marek, explained clearly what needed replacing and showed me the worn pads. The job took about two hours and the price matched the estimate — 680 PLN all in. No surprises. The waiting area is clean and there's decent coffee. I'll be back for the next service."
> "I've been using this pharmacy for about two years now. The pharmacist always takes time to explain drug interactions, which I really appreciate given that I take three different prescriptions. Twice I've had them flag a potential issue that my doctor missed. The prices are mid-range and waiting times are usually under 10 minutes."

---

### SPECIFIC_DETAILS — names, products, prices, waiting times, or physical descriptions (real signal)

> "Asked for Michał specifically — he diagnosed the rattling noise in under 10 minutes. Turned out to be a loose heat shield. Fixed on the spot, no charge."
> "Waited about 35 minutes for a table on a Saturday, but the lamb chops at 68 PLN were worth it."
> "The Samsung Galaxy screen replacement took exactly 90 minutes as promised. Cost was 280 PLN, same as quoted on the phone."

---

### BALANCED_TONE — positives and negatives or caveats (real signal)

> "The food was genuinely good and portions were generous. Service was a bit slow but the staff were friendly about it. Would come back, just not on a busy evening."
> "Quality of the repair is excellent. My only complaint is the parking — very limited spots and the entrance is hard to find the first time."

---

### NATURAL_LANGUAGE — typos, informal tone, personal voice (real signal)

> "not the first time here, always fine. this time waited a bit longer than usual but no big deal, they did a good job"
> "went here after my regular garage couldnt fit me in. bit hard to find (park behind the supermarket not in front) but worth it"